// Repro 10 — GITI-014: zero-arg arrow returning an object literal loses
// its wrapping parens in client-side emit of reactive-state defaults.
//
// Source: `@var = { ... }` declares a reactive variable with an object
// initializer (spec §6.6).
//
// The compiler emits a paired init:
//   _scrml_reactive_set("var", _scrml_deep_reactive({...}));   // OK
//   _scrml_init_set("var", () => {...});                       // BROKEN — needs ({...})
//
// The 2nd line is a zero-arg arrow whose body is meant to be the object
// literal. Without wrapping parens, JS parses `{ error: null, ... }` as
// a block statement with labelled statements, not as an expression
// returning an object.
//
// This is the same structural defect that GITI-013 closed for the
// single-arg case `f => ({...})` (commit `0af4eaf`). The zero-arg path
// `() => ({...})` wasn't covered.
//
// To reproduce:
//   bun run compiler/src/cli.js compile ui/repros/repro-10-zero-arg-arrow-object-init.scrml -o /tmp/r10
//   grep "_scrml_init_set" /tmp/r10/repro-10-zero-arg-arrow-object-init.client.js
//     -> shows `_scrml_init_set("probe", () => {error: not, count: 0});`
//   bun --check /tmp/r10/repro-10-zero-arg-arrow-object-init.client.js
//     -> SyntaxError: Expected ";" but found ":"
//
// Runtime impact: every page that declares a reactive variable with an
// object initializer fails to parse its client bundle. All five giti UI
// pages currently regress — no reactive state ever initializes, awaited
// fetches never wire to the DOM, pages render as empty defaults forever.

<program>

${
  @probe = { error: not, count: 0 }
}

<div>
  <p>zero-arg arrow probe — error=${@probe.error}, count=${@probe.count}</p>
</div>

</program>
